 *      same frame, before React has re-rendered the button as disabled, is
 *      dropped rather than sent.
 *   2. The action returns the new job's id; the hook then follows THAT job
 *      through the page's real, server-rendered `Job` rows (refreshed by the
 *      page's existing <AutoRefresh/> while anything is in flight) — never a
 *      local timer. A GAME_POWER start legitimately takes from seconds to
 *      several minutes depending on the game (worker/game-control.ts
 *      `powerStartTimeoutMs`), plus up to two worker retries.
 *   3. A job already in flight when the page loads (a reload, another tab, a
 *      scheduled restart) is adopted from the snapshot, so a fresh page never
 *      shows enabled buttons for a server that is mid-action.
 *   4. When the job ends: a clear success/failure outcome, then a short
 *      cooldown before the controls come back, so the reflexive click that
 *      lands the instant the state flips does not fire a new action.
